Title: Build ffmpeg's AC-3 and E-AC-3 decoders
Origin: Thorium (other/add-ac3-eac3-ffmpeg-decoders.patch), ported with a fix

The audio counterpart to 12-add-hevc-ffmpeg-decoder-parser.patch, and the
reason HEVC is worth having: a 4K HEVC file on the web is usually carrying
AC-3 or E-AC-3 audio, and a video that decodes to silence is not much better
than one that does not decode. Chromium leaves these to the platform for the
same licensing reasons it leaves HEVC there.

Applies on top of patch 12 -- both edit the same generated configs and the same
ffmpeg_generated.gni blocks, so the order in series matters.

  chromium/config/Chrome/*/*/config.h
                          CONFIG_AC3DSP and CONFIG_FMTCONVERT.
  .../config_components.h CONFIG_AC3_DECODER, CONFIG_AC3_FIXED_DECODER,
                          CONFIG_EAC3_DECODER, CONFIG_AC3_PARSER,
                          CONFIG_AC3_DEMUXER, CONFIG_EAC3_DEMUXER,
                          CONFIG_EAC3_CORE_BSF.
  .../libavcodec/codec_list.c, .../parser_list.c
                          ff_ac3_decoder, ff_eac3_decoder,
                          ff_ac3_fixed_decoder, ff_ac3_parser.
  ffmpeg_generated.gni    libavcodec/ac3*, libavformat/ac3dec.c, and the x86 and
                          aarch64 ac3dsp.

Unlike HEVC, which this series enables everywhere, AC-3 lands in 9 of the 18
configs -- the ones Thorium builds: win/{x64,ia32,arm64}, linux/{x64,ia32,arm64},
mac/{x64,arm64} and android/arm64. The rest keep it off, which is upstream's
state, so no decision of ours is hidden in those files.

Two notes on what came across from Thorium:

fmtconvert, which is the fix in "ported with a fix". ffmpeg's configure says
  ac3_decoder_select="ac3_parser ac3dsp bswapdsp fmtconvert"
and Thorium's patch enables the first three. ac3dec_float.c includes ac3dec.c,
which calls ff_fmt_convert_init() with no CONFIG guard around it, so every
platform that compiles the file fails at link time:
  lld-link: error: undefined symbol: ff_fmt_convert_init
  >>> referenced by third_party/ffmpeg/libavcodec/ac3dec_float.c
This patch therefore also sets CONFIG_FMTCONVERT in those 9 configs and adds
libavcodec/fmtconvert.c plus the per-arch init that fmtconvert.c dispatches to
-- x86, aarch64, arm and riscv, taken from libavcodec/*/Makefile. All four are
needed, not just x86: ac3dec_float.c is added in the block that covers every
Chrome config, so fmtconvert.c compiles on arm and riscv64 too and wants its
ff_fmt_convert_init_arm / _riscv there. Worth reporting upstream to Thorium,
where the same link failure is latent on all nine platforms.

The AudioToolbox macros (CONFIG_AC3_AT_DECODER, CONFIG_EAC3_AT_DECODER) are
flipped to 1 in every config Thorium touched, including the Windows and Linux
ones. They are inert here -- the GN build takes its sources from
ffmpeg_generated.gni and its registrations from codec_list.c, and neither names
audiotoolboxdec.c or ff_ac3_at_decoder -- so this keeps them rather than
introducing a second kind of difference from Thorium to maintain.

Verified by building third_party/ffmpeg:ffmpeg for win/x64: ffmpeg.dll carries
"ATSC A/52A (AC-3)" and "ATSC A/52B (AC-3, E-AC-3)", and fmtconvert.obj is in
the link.

